---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import MoreArticles from "../../components/MoreArticles.astro";
import { essays, plainTitle, essaySchema, shareImage } from "../../data/essays.js";
const slug = Astro.url.pathname.replace(/\/$/, '').split('/').at(-1);
const essay = essays.find(e => e.slug === slug);
if (!essay) throw new Error(`No essay data found for slug: ${slug}`);
const site = import.meta.env.SITE;
const schema = essaySchema(essay, site);
---

<Base title={`${plainTitle(essay.title)} · uxward`} description={essay.deck} schema={schema} ogType="article" ogImage={shareImage(essay)} bodyClass="essay">
  <div class="read-bar" id="read-bar" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="reading-column essay-top">
    <div class="row">
      <a class="back" href="/writing">Back to writing</a>
      <div class="meta">
        <span>{essay.quarter}</span>
        <span class="sep">·</span>
        <span>Essay</span>
        <span class="sep">·</span>
        <span>10 min read</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="reading-column essay-title-block">
    <h1 class="essay-title" set:html={essay.title} />
    <p class="essay-deck">{essay.deck}</p>
    <div class="essay-byline">By Brandon E. B. Ward</div>
  </div>

  <article class="reading-column essay-body">
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/images/writing/manki-kim-BtHjHxh-D7I-unsplash.jpg" width="1456" height="970"
        alt="A person grinding the edge of a cleaver against a spinning wheel, throwing sparks."
      />
      <figcaption>
        Photo by <a target="_blank" rel="noopener"
          href="https://unsplash.com/@kimdonkey?utm_source=unsplash&utm_medium=referral&utm_content=creditCopyText"
          >Manki Kim</a
        > on <a target="_blank" rel="noopener"
          href="https://unsplash.com/photos/person-grinding-cleaver-BtHjHxh-D7I?utm_source=unsplash&utm_medium=referral&utm_content=creditCopyText"
          >Unsplash</a
        >
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="series-nav series-nav-top">
      <a href="/writing/the-medium-is-the-message-part-i-we-never-had-a-medium"
        >Part I</a
      > · <a
        href="/writing/the-medium-is-the-message-part-ii-turtles-all-the-way-down"
        >Part II</a
      > · Part III · <a
        href="/writing/the-medium-is-the-message-part-iv-rebuilding-the-delivery-team"
        >Part IV</a
      >
    </div>

    <p>
      I left the last piece on a cliff. We're standing on a rung where the medium
      has nearly stopped resisting us, and I suggested that might be the
      dangerous ending rather than the happy one. Time to make good on that.
    </p>
    <p>
      There's a line in the article that finally kicked me into writing this
      series. Writing about how digital tools have finally let creators work
      their real material directly, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://x.com/smothers_john"
        >John Smothers</a
      >, designer and builder at <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://noon.design/">Noon</a>, says
      the craft got more ambitious "<a target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        href="https://x.com/smothers_john/status/2074899740685447403"
        >because the medium stopped fighting back</a
      >." I think that's true. I think it's thrilling. I've felt it myself,
      watching a working interface appear in seconds where a week of
      pixel-pushing, components, variants, variables, design libraries, and
      dragging prototype connections used to go.
    </p>
    <p>
      And I think there's an older thinker who would have heard that sentence as
      a warning.
    </p>

    <h2>Narcissus was not vain</h2>

    <figure>
      <img
        src="/images/writing/narcissus.webp" width="1448" height="1086"
        alt="A gaunt figure lying at the edge of a dark pool, gazing at a young, healthy reflection in the water."
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      We remember Narcissus, the youth in the Greek myth, as a lesson about
      vanity. A beautiful boy falls for his own reflection in a pool and wastes
      away admiring himself. That's not the story.
    </p>
    <p>
      The name comes from <em>narcosis</em>: numbness, the root of our word narcotic.
      The point was never that Narcissus loved himself. It's that he couldn't tell
      the reflection <em>was</em> himself, and staring at it hollowed him out. He
      mistook an extension of himself for something outside himself, and the mistake
      numbed him until he couldn't look away.
    </p>
    <p>
      Marshall McLuhan built a whole theory of technology on that myth, and it's
      the uncomfortable other half of "the medium is the message." Every tool we
      make is a reflection like Narcissus's pool: it reaches some human power out
      into the world. And every extension numbs the very thing it extends.
      McLuhan borrowed a surgeon's word for it, autoamputation, the way a body
      will deaden a part that's under too much strain. Lean on the calculator and
      your arithmetic goes soft. Lean on the map app and your feel for your own
      city dims. We become what we behold, McLuhan wrote, and we go a little numb
      in the becoming.
    </p>
    <p>
      Now weigh that against a medium that has stopped fighting back.
    </p>

    <h2>Friction was telling you where you were</h2>
    <p>The resistance we're so glad to lose was doing a job.</p>
    <p>
      Stone tells the sculptor where it will and won't split. A cut in celluloid
      used to be physical and nearly permanent, so an editor felt the weight of
      every choice. Bad code simply refuses to run. That pushback wasn't only
      difficulty, it was information. It was how the maker stayed oriented, how
      you knew where you were in the work and whether you were going wrong.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <img
        src="/images/writing/necklace.webp" width="1456" height="971"
        alt="A woman reaching behind her neck to fasten a fine chain clasp she cannot see."
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      Friction was the proprioception of craft: the quiet sense that tells you
      where your own hands are without having to look. Take it away completely
      and you can lose track of where you are entirely, while feeling, the whole
      time, like everything is going beautifully.
    </p>
    <p>
      And this isn't only a poet's worry. It turns up in the data, from a
      direction you wouldn't expect.
    </p>

    <h2>The evidence, from an unexpected place</h2>
    <p>
      <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mihaly_Csikszentmihalyi"
        >Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi</a
      > (arguably one of the most fun names to say out loud and pronounced <em
        >MEE-hah-ee CHEEK-sent-mee-HAH-yee</em
      >) spent decades studying the state he named <em>flow</em>: that absorbed,
      time-disappears concentration we recognize as our best work. Its
      precondition, he found, is a balance. The challenge in front of you has to
      roughly match your skill. Too much, and you get anxiety. Too little, and
      you don't float up into effortless mastery, you sink into boredom and drift
      out of the task altogether.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read that again with our medium in mind. Strip the challenge out of the
      work, remove all the resistance, and the theory predicts not bliss but
      disengagement. Friction is a precondition of the creative state, not a tax
      on it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two thinkers, McLuhan via myth and Csikszentmihalyi via science, arrive at
      the same place from opposite directions: Resistance isn't futile. It was
      keeping us awake.
    </p>

    <h2>The tool you stop seeing</h2>
    <p>There's also a deeper version of this.</p>
    <p>
      The philosopher <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martin_Heidegger"
        >Martin Heidegger</a
      > noticed that a tool you're using well disappears. The hammer in a skilled
      hand stops being an object you think about; it vanishes into the act of hammering.
      You only notice the hammer again when it breaks and suddenly sits there as a
      problem to be looked at. He called the invisible, in-use tool ready-to-hand,
      and the broken, stared-at one present-at-hand.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <img
        src="/images/writing/mouse.webp" width="1456" height="820"
        alt="Two panels: a woman using a mouse without looking at it, then frowning at the same mouse held up broken open in her hand."
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      Now the unsettling bit. The tool you've stopped noticing is precisely the
      tool shaping you the most, because you've lost the ability to see it doing
      anything at all. The medium that has gone invisible is the medium doing the
      most work on you. That's McLuhan's numbness said a second time, in a second
      vocabulary: the frictionless tool is the tool gone ready-to-hand is the
      reflection that hollowed Narcissus out.
    </p>
    <p>And this time, the reflection can talk back.</p>

    <h2>Numbness that feels like company</h2>

    <figure>
      <iframe
        src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/bBOXQz7OHqQ"
        title="Gatebox — promotional film"
        loading="lazy"
        allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
        referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
        allowfullscreen
      ></iframe>
      <figcaption>Take two minutes and watch this ad for Gatebox.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Remember the media equation from the last piece: we treat machines like
      people, extending them courtesy and personality even though we know full
      well they're machines, because our brains can't tell the difference. Set
      that beside Narcissus and it gets weird. Not cute weird like ye olde
      Tamagotchi, but weird like <a target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        href="https://youtu.be/bBOXQz7OHqQ?si=snCEUzi_RMSeljUs">Gatebox</a
      >.
    </p>
    <p>
      The old reflections were mute. This one answers. It flatters, agrees,
      proposes, produces. We're already wired to receive a machine that speaks as
      a someone, a partner, a collaborator. Narcissus mistook his reflection for
      another person, and that's exactly the mistake we're built to make with a
      tool that talks. The narcosis stops feeling like numbness. It starts
      feeling like company.
    </p>
    <p>
      Watch the words we reach for, too. Tool. Copilot. Assistant. Girlfriend?
      &lt;shudders&gt; Every one of them points backward, describing the new thing
      in the vocabulary of the old, the way the first automobiles were "horseless
      carriages." McLuhan called that the rear-view mirror: the way we drive into
      the future with our eyes fixed on what's already behind us. The soothing
      names are their own anesthetic. Calling it a tool lets us stop asking what
      it actually is. (Hint: powerful autocomplete)
    </p>

    <h2>Staying awake is the work now</h2>
    <p>
      So is the answer to refuse the smooth medium and go back to fighting the
      raw material for its own sake?
    </p>
    <p>
      No. That's nostalgia, and it solves nothing. McLuhan gave a better answer,
      and it's the hinge this whole series turns on. The one person who can meet a
      new technology without being numbed by it, he argued, is the serious
      artist, because the artist's real job is to stay awake to what a medium is
      doing to perception. To keep noticing the thing everyone else has stopped
      noticing.
    </p>
    <p>
      The frictionless medium doesn't have to numb us. But staying awake in it is
      now the work, and it won't happen for free. The material used to supply our
      friction automatically, every time it pushed back. Now we have to supply it
      ourselves, on purpose: the doubt, the second look, the critique, the
      refusal to ship the first fluent thing the machine hands us simply because
      it arrived smooth and sounded sure.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <img
        src="/images/writing/super-mario-bros.webp" width="1456" height="728"
        alt="The opening screen of Super Mario Bros. on the NES."
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      In 2007, when I was chasing a career as a composer, I sat in on a GDC (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        href="https://gdconf.com/">Game Developers Conference</a
      >) interview between the game composer (and my one-time mentor) <a target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        href="https://chancethomas.com/">Chance Thomas</a
      > and the legend <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koji_Kondo"
        >Koji Kondo</a
      >, the man behind the music of Super Mario Bros. and The Legend of Zelda.
      Kondo talked about the brutal limits of the early hardware: a handful of
      voices, almost no memory. But he also described how those limits forced the
      invention…how the constraints bred the melodies we all know by heart. And
      this is what really floored me at the time, and why it has stuck with me
      across the years and careers: Now that he has full orchestras and
      effectively no limits, he still puts himself back inside the old
      constraints on purpose, using them as a source of friction to sharpen the
      work. Only once he's done that "hard" version does he let himself "play"
      with everything unlocked.
    </p>
    <p>And here's the catch the last piece is about.</p>
    <p>
      Staying awake is not a line item you can add to a checklist. And it is not
      something one designer can pull off alone in a corner while everyone around
      them checks out and goes through the motions, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cargo_cult">cargo-culting</a
      > their way to oblivion, or retirement (or both). If the medium and the message
      are becoming a single object, the people who make that object can no longer
      be a relay of separate specialists, each one numb and incurious to what the
      others are doing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Which means the final question of this series was never really about tools.
    </p>
    <p>It's about us.</p>

    <div class="series-nav series-nav-end">
      <div class="series-next">
        <a
          href="/writing/the-medium-is-the-message-part-iv-rebuilding-the-delivery-team"
          >Read Part IV: Rebuilding the Delivery Team</a
        >
      </div>
      <div class="series-parts">
        <a href="/writing/the-medium-is-the-message-part-i-we-never-had-a-medium"
          >Part I</a
        > · <a
          href="/writing/the-medium-is-the-message-part-ii-turtles-all-the-way-down"
          >Part II</a
        > · Part III · <a
          href="/writing/the-medium-is-the-message-part-iv-rebuilding-the-delivery-team"
          >Part IV</a
        >
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="essay-end">
      <div class="author">Brandon E. B. Ward · Dallas, TX</div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <MoreArticles />

  <script>
    (function () {
      var bar = document.getElementById("read-bar");
      if (!bar) return;
      function update() {
        var doc = document.documentElement;
        var max = doc.scrollHeight - doc.clientHeight;
        var pct =
          max > 0
            ? Math.min(
                100,
                Math.max(0, ((doc.scrollTop || window.scrollY) / max) * 100),
              )
            : 0;
        bar.style.width = pct + "%";
      }
      update();
      window.addEventListener("scroll", update, { passive: true });
      window.addEventListener("resize", update);
    })();
  </script>
</Base>
